INSERT INTO search_index (course_id, entity_type, entity_id, url, title, body) VALUES ('course_computer_vision', 'lesson', 'les_d210cceea941f3d2', '/courses/computer-vision/lectures/computer-vision-tasks-and-applications', 'Computer vision tasks and applications', 'Follow the five steps of a computer vision pipeline, tell apart the main tasks (classification, detection, segmentation, face recognition, tracking), and see where computer vision is used, from self-driving cars to farms. Ends with the course roadmap. Name the five steps of a basic computer vision pipeline in order Tell image classification, object detection and image segmentation apart by their output Describe face recognition and motion tracking in one sentence each Give examples of computer vision applications, including autonomous vehicles The basic computer vision pipeline Most computer vision systems work in the same five steps, one after the other: Step What happens Example: a car''s camera --- --- --- 1. Image acquisition a camera captures the image one frame of the road ahead 2. Image processing the image is cleaned and prepared make it the right size, reduce noise 3. Feature extraction simple, useful patterns are found edges, corners, lane-line pixels 4. Analysis the patterns are interpreted "these edges are the lane; that shape is a car" 5. Decision the system acts on what it found keep in lane, slow down The labs in this course follow the same order: the next lecture is about step 2 (improving images) and the one after is about step 3 (features). Five steps from image to decision The main computer vision tasks Task What it gives you --- --- Image classification one label for the whole image Object detection each object''s label and location (a box) Image segmentation a label for every pixel Face recognition who a face belongs to Motion tracking where an object moves from frame to frame The first three answer more and more detailed questions about the same image. Classification asks "what is this a picture of?", detection asks "what objects are here, and where?", and segmentation asks "which object does each pixel belong to?". Classification, detection and segmentation These outputs were made from the drawing itself: the program that drew street.png knows where every object is. A real system has to work them out from the pixels, and that is what the later lectures build towards. Classification, detection, segmentation Image classification takes an input image and gives back a class label : input image → computer vision model → "car". It says what the image shows, but not where. Object detection answers two questions: what objects are present, and where they are. Each object gets a box and a label. A real detector also gives each box a confidence score , a number from 0 to 1 that says how sure it is (0.97 means very sure). The pictures above have no scores, because their boxes come from the drawing, not from a detector. Image segmentation classifies every pixel of the image, so it finds the exact region of each object, not just a box around it. A self-driving car uses this to know exactly which pixels are road. Face recognition and motion tracking - Face recognition finds faces and identifies or verifies who each one is, as when a phone unlocks for its owner. - Motion tracking follows objects across video frames : the same car in frame 1, frame 2, frame 3, so the system knows where it is going. Where is computer vision used? Application One example --- --- Autonomous vehicles find lanes, cars, pedestrians and signs Medical imaging point out suspicious areas in X-rays and scans Security and surveillance notice a person entering a restricted area Robotics let a robot arm see where to grab an object Manufacturing spot faulty products on a production line Facial recognition unlock a phone, check identity at a gate Agriculture find diseased plants or count fruit from a drone Computer vision in autonomous vehicles A self-driving car runs the pipeline many times a second: Camera → computer vision → understanding the environment → vehicle decision From the camera''s view of the road, the computer vision system must find: - cars and pedestrians , which move and must be avoided; - traffic signs and traffic lights , which say what is allowed; - lane lines , which say where the car should drive. Only when all of these are understood can the car decide to continue, turn, slow down or stop. This course''s semester project builds a small version of exactly this system. What a car''s camera has to find Our drawing has no traffic light, but every other item on the list is there. Again, the boxes and highlighted lanes come from the drawing, to show what a working system must produce. Course roadmap What we will explore this semester: 1. Image enhancement and restoration 2. Feature detection and matching 3. Object detection 4. Deep learning for image classification 5. Semantic segmentation 6. 3D reconstruction 7. Motion analysis 8. Visual SLAM (building a map while finding where the camera is) 9. Vision-based navigation Each topic adds one more ability, and the semester project puts them together on a vehicle. Check yourself (a) Image classification : one label for the whole image. (b) Object detection : a label and a box for each object. (c) Image segmentation : a label for every pixel. Feature extraction (step 3). Image processing (step 2) only prepares the image, for example by resizing it or removing noise. Common mistakes - Mixing up detection and segmentation. Detection gives a box around each object; segmentation gives the exact pixels . - Thinking classification says where. A classifier says "car" for the whole image, not where the car is. - Skipping step 2. A dark or noisy image makes every later step worse. That is why the next lecture is about improving images. Key takeaways - The pipeline: acquisition → processing → feature extraction → analysis → decision . - Classification gives one label, detection gives labels with boxes, segmentation labels every pixel. - Face recognition says who; motion tracking follows objects across frames. - Self-driving cars must find cars, pedestrians, signs, traffic lights and lane lines before they decide.');
INSERT INTO lessons (id, course_id, topic_id, slug, title, description, lecture_number, sort_order, difficulty, objectives, prerequisites, reading_minutes, status, published_at, updated_at) VALUES ('les_c5f7f82402451f6e', 'course_computer_vision', 'top_002afa2197637fed', 'reading-and-showing-images', 'Getting started with OpenCV', 'Install OpenCV, read an image from a file, show it in a window and print its shape. The first step of every lab, and the start of the image enhancement lecture.', 2, 3, 'easy', '["Install OpenCV with pip and import it as cv2","Read an image with cv2.imread and check that it loaded","Show an image with cv2.imshow, cv2.waitKey and cv2.destroyAllWindows","Read an image''s height, width and channels from img.shape"]', '["What is computer vision?"]', 6, 'published', 1792022400000, 1792022400000) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET topic_id = excluded.topic_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lecture_number = excluded.lecture_number, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objectives = excluded.objectives, prerequisites = excluded.prerequisites, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = excluded.status, published_at = coalesce(lessons.published_at, excluded.published_at), updated_at = excluded.updated_at;
DELETE FROM lesson_sections WHERE course_id = 'course_computer_vision' AND lesson_id = 'les_c5f7f82402451f6e';
DELETE FROM lesson_relations WHERE course_id = 'course_computer_vision' AND lesson_id = 'les_c5f7f82402451f6e';
INSERT INTO lesson_sections (id, course_id, lesson_id, kind, title, body_md, code, code_language, runnable, sample_input, expected_output, annotations, image_key, image_alt, image_width, image_height, sort_order, updated_at) VALUES ('sec_6c02f3c581dd454c', 'course_computer_vision', 'les_c5f7f82402451f6e', 'explanation', 'If an image is only numbers…', 'Last lecture ended with a question: if an image is only numbers, **can we change the image by changing these numbers?** Yes. That is what this lecture does, with Python and OpenCV, in eleven steps:

| Step | You will… |
|---|---|
| 1 | read an image |
| 2 | look at its properties |
| 3 | convert it to grayscale |
| 4 | resize it |
| 5, 6 | change its brightness and contrast |
| 7, 8 | blur it and remove noise |
| 9 | sharpen it |
| 10 | improve its contrast automatically (histogram equalization) |
| 11 | solve a mini challenge: fix a bad image |', NULL, NULL, 0, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 1792022400000);
INSERT INTO lesson_sections (id, course_id, lesson_id, kind, title, body_md, code, code_language, runnable, sample_input, expected_output, annotations, image_key, image_alt, image_width, image_height, sort_order, updated_at) VALUES ('sec_00fa05c9fefa3736', 'course_computer_vision', 'les_c5f7f82402451f6e', 'explanation', 'Getting started with OpenCV', '**OpenCV** (Open Source Computer Vision Library) is a free library with thousands of image functions. Install it once, from a terminal (Command Prompt on Windows):

```text
pip install opencv-python
```

Then, in every Python file that uses it:

```python
import cv2
```

`cv2` is the module name you will write in front of every OpenCV function. These are the ones this lecture uses:

| Function | What it does |
|---|---|
| `cv2.imread()` | loads an image from a file |
| `cv2.imshow()` | displays an image in a window |
| `cv2.resize()` | changes an image''s size |
| `cv2.cvtColor()` | converts between colour formats |
| `cv2.GaussianBlur()` | smooths an image |
| `cv2.equalizeHist()` | improves contrast automatically |

OpenCV programs open windows on your screen, so they run on your own computer, not in the browser. The programs on these pages show their recorded output instead of a Run button.', NULL, NULL, 0, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 2, 1792022400000);
INSERT INTO lesson_sections (id, course_id, lesson_id, kind, title, body_md, code, code_language, runnable, sample_input, expected_output, annotations, image_key, image_alt, image_width, image_height, sort_order, updated_at) VALUES ('sec_1f25122fa01a540c', 'course_computer_vision', 'les_c5f7f82402451f6e', 'diagram', 'The image used in these labs', 'This is `street.png`, 960 pixels wide and 540 pixels tall. Download it with the link under the picture and save it **in the same folder as your Python file**. You can also use a photo of your own: change the file name in the code.', NULL, NULL, 0, '', NULL, NULL, 'courses/course_computer_vision/lessons/les_c5f7f82402451f6e/street.png', 'A drawn street scene, 960 by 540 pixels: four city buildings with grids of windows on the left, two grey mountains with snow caps, a road with white edge lines and a dashed yellow centre line, a red car, a person crossing, a stop sign, a house and a tree on the right', 960, 540, 3, 1792022400000);
INSERT INTO lesson_sections (id, course_id, lesson_id, kind, title, body_md, code, code_language, runnable, sample_input, expected_output, annotations, image_key, image_alt, image_width, image_height, sort_order, updated_at) VALUES ('sec_f01285c25a20652f', 'course_computer_vision', 'les_c5f7f82402451f6e', 'code', 'Step 1: read and show an image', '', 'import cv2

img = cv2.imread("street.png")
cv2.imshow("Original Image", img)
cv2.waitKey(0)
cv2.destroyAllWindows()', 'python', 0, '', NULL, '{"notes":[{"line":3,"match":"imread","text":"loads the image","shape":"circle","color":"sky"},{"line":4,"match":"imshow","text":"displays it","shape":"square","color":"yel"},{"line":5,"match":"waitKey(0)","text":"waits for a key","shape":"diamond","color":"green"},{"line":6,"match":"destroyAllWindows","text":"closes the windows","shape":"tri","color":"coral"}],"strips":[]}', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 4, 1792022400000);
INSERT INTO lesson_sections (id, course_id, lesson_id, kind, title, body_md, code, code_language, runnable, sample_input, expected_output, annotations, image_key, image_alt, image_width, image_height, sort_order, updated_at) VALUES ('sec_15a238dcdfd3aadc', 'course_computer_vision', 'les_c5f7f82402451f6e', 'explanation', 'What each line does', '| Line | What it does |
|---|---|
| `img = cv2.imread("street.png")` | **loads** the image from the file into the variable `img` |
| `cv2.imshow("Original Image", img)` | **displays** `img` in a window whose title is "Original Image" |
| `cv2.waitKey(0)` | **waits** until you press any key; 0 means "wait forever" |
| `cv2.destroyAllWindows()` | **closes** all the windows OpenCV opened |

Without `waitKey()`, the window would open and close in a blink, or stay grey and frozen: OpenCV only draws its windows while it waits for a key.', NULL, NULL, 0, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 5, 1792022400000);
INSERT INTO lesson_sections (id, course_id, lesson_id, kind, title, body_md, code, code_language, runnable, sample_input, expected_output, annotations, image_key, image_alt, image_width, image_height, sort_order, updated_at) VALUES ('sec_71446838f6b81504', 'course_computer_vision', 'les_c5f7f82402451f6e', 'diagram', 'The window it opens', 'Press any key while the window is selected to close it and end the program.', NULL, NULL, 0, '', NULL, NULL, 'courses/course_computer_vision/lessons/les_c5f7f82402451f6e/cv-window-original.png', 'A window titled Original Image showing the street scene', 1018, 627, 6, 1792022400000);
INSERT INTO lesson_sections (id, course_id, lesson_id, kind, title, body_md, code, code_language, runnable, sample_input, expected_output, annotations, image_key, image_alt, image_width, image_height, sort_order, updated_at) VALUES ('sec_84c7e406e51b3c6c', 'course_computer_vision', 'les_c5f7f82402451f6e', 'code', 'Did the image load?', 'If the file name is wrong, or the file isn''t in the same folder, `imread()` does **not** stop with an error. It returns `None`, and the program fails one line later with a confusing message. Check straight after reading. (OpenCV may also print its own warning line about the file.)', 'import cv2

img = cv2.imread("stret.png")
if img is None:
    print("Could not read the image. Check the file name and folder.")
else:
    print("Loaded:", img.shape)', 'python', 0, '', 'Could not read the image. Check the file name and folder.
', '{"notes":[{"line":3,"match":"stret.png","text":"a typo in the name","shape":"tri","color":"coral"},{"line":4,"match":"img is None","text":"nothing was loaded","shape":"circle","color":"sky"}],"strips":[]}', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 7, 1792022400000);
INSERT INTO lesson_sections (id, course_id, lesson_id, kind, title, body_md, code, code_language, runnable, sample_input, expected_output, annotations, image_key, image_alt, image_width, image_height, sort_order, updated_at) VALUES ('sec_db3c01e4bf512447', 'course_computer_vision', 'les_c5f7f82402451f6e', 'explanation', 'Step 2: images are arrays', '`img` is a NumPy **array**: a grid of numbers with a **shape**. Print the shape to learn the image''s size:

```python
print(img.shape)
```

For `street.png` it prints `(540, 960, 3)`:

| Number | Meaning |
|---|---|
| 540 | **height**: the number of rows of pixels |
| 960 | **width**: the number of columns |
| 3 | **colour channels**: three numbers per pixel |

The order is always **height × width × channels**. Height comes first.', NULL, NULL, 0, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 8, 1792022400000);
INSERT INTO lesson_sections (id, course_id, lesson_id, kind, title, body_md, code, code_language, runnable, sample_input, expected_output, annotations, image_key, image_alt, image_width, image_height, sort_order, updated_at) VALUES ('sec_5c7b3b0d6cbfdddc', 'course_computer_vision', 'les_c5f7f82402451f6e', 'code', 'Printing the properties', '', 'import cv2

img = cv2.imread("street.png")
print(img.shape)
height, width, channels = img.shape
print("Width:", width)
print("Height:", height)
print("Channels:", channels)', 'python', 0, '', '(540, 960, 3)
Width: 960
Height: 540
Channels: 3
', '{"notes":[{"line":4,"match":"img.shape","text":"(height, width, channels)","shape":"circle","color":"sky"},{"line":5,"match":"height, width, channels","text":"three variables at once","shape":"square","color":"yel"}],"strips":[]}', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 9, 1792022400000);
INSERT INTO lesson_sections (id, course_id, lesson_id, kind, title, body_md, code, code_language, runnable, sample_input, expected_output, annotations, image_key, image_alt, image_width, image_height, sort_order, updated_at) VALUES ('sec_a7777e44e1089b46', 'course_computer_vision', 'les_c5f7f82402451f6e', 'explanation', 'OpenCV uses BGR, not RGB', 'The three channels are **B**lue, **G**reen and **R**ed, **in that order**. Most other tools say RGB, so OpenCV''s order surprises everyone once:

| Channel | Colour |
|---|---|
| 0 | B: blue |
| 1 | G: green |
| 2 | R: red |

So in OpenCV `(0, 0, 255)` is **red** and `(255, 0, 0)` is **blue**. The next lesson looks at single pixels in detail.', NULL, NULL, 0, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 10, 1792022400000);
INSERT INTO lesson_sections (id, course_id, lesson_id, kind, title, body_md, code, code_language, runnable, sample_input, expected_output, annotations, image_key, image_alt, image_width, image_height, sort_order, updated_at) VALUES ('sec_82aa614e4fb13151', 'course_computer_vision', 'les_c5f7f82402451f6e', 'explanation', 'Student task 1: try it yourself', 'Write this in your own Python file:

1. Choose any image (a photo from your phone works).
2. Load it with OpenCV.
3. Display it.
4. Print its shape.

Then answer: **What is the width? What is the height? How many channels?** Remember that the shape lists the height first.', NULL, NULL, 0, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 11, 1792022400000);
INSERT INTO lesson_sections (id, course_id, lesson_id, kind, title, body_md, code, code_language, runnable, sample_input, expected_output, annotations, image_key, image_alt, image_width, image_height, sort_order, updated_at) VALUES ('sec_3d9e30d1916ef2b2', 'course_computer_vision', 'les_c5f7f82402451f6e', 'note', 'Check yourself', '::: check A program prints `(720, 1280, 3)` for `img.shape`. How wide is the image?
???
**1280 pixels.** The shape is (height, width, channels), so the height is 720 and the width is 1280.
:::', NULL, NULL, 0, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 12, 1792022400000);
INSERT INTO lesson_sections (id, course_id, lesson_id, kind, title, body_md, code, code_language, runnable, sample_input, expected_output, annotations, image_key, image_alt, image_width, image_height, sort_order, updated_at) VALUES ('sec_45c10c19a64cdac4', 'course_computer_vision', 'les_c5f7f82402451f6e', 'common_mistakes', 'Common mistakes', '- **The image is not in the same folder.** `imread()` returns `None`; check with `if img is None:`.
- **Forgetting `waitKey()`.** The window flashes or stays grey. Always call `cv2.waitKey(0)` after `imshow()`.
- **Reading the shape as width × height.** It is **height × width × channels**.
- **Expecting RGB.** OpenCV stores **BGR**: red is `(0, 0, 255)`.
- **Naming your own file `cv2.py`.** Then `import cv2` imports your file instead of OpenCV. Give your files other names.', NULL, NULL, 0, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 13, 1792022400000);
INSERT INTO lesson_sections (id, course_id, lesson_id, kind, title, body_md, code, code_language, runnable, sample_input, expected_output, annotations, image_key, image_alt, image_width, image_height, sort_order, updated_at) VALUES ('sec_b204be3ac9f27289', 'course_computer_vision', 'les_c5f7f82402451f6e', 'key_takeaways', 'Key takeaways', '- Install with `pip install opencv-python`; use it with `import cv2`.
- `imread` loads, `imshow` displays, `waitKey` waits for a key, `destroyAllWindows` closes.
- `imread` returns `None` for a missing file, so check it.
- `img.shape` is **(height, width, channels)**, and colour images are stored as **BGR**.', NULL, NULL, 0, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 14, 1792022400000);
DELETE FROM search_index WHERE course_id = 'course_computer_vision' AND entity_type = 'lesson' AND entity_id = 'les_c5f7f82402451f6e';
INSERT INTO search_index (course_id, entity_type, entity_id, url, title, body) VALUES ('course_computer_vision', 'lesson', 'les_c5f7f82402451f6e', '/courses/computer-vision/lectures/reading-and-showing-images', 'Getting started with OpenCV', 'Install OpenCV, read an image from a file, show it in a window and print its shape. The first step of every lab, and the start of the image enhancement lecture. Install OpenCV with pip and import it as cv2 Read an image with cv2.imread and check that it loaded Show an image with cv2.imshow, cv2.waitKey and cv2.destroyAllWindows Read an image''s height, width and channels from img.shape If an image is only numbers… Last lecture ended with a question: if an image is only numbers, can we change the image by changing these numbers? Yes. That is what this lecture does, with Python and OpenCV, in eleven steps: Step You will… --- --- 1 read an image 2 look at its properties 3 convert it to grayscale 4 resize it 5, 6 change its brightness and contrast 7, 8 blur it and remove noise 9 sharpen it 10 improve its contrast automatically (histogram equalization) 11 solve a mini challenge: fix a bad image Getting started with OpenCV OpenCV (Open Source Computer Vision Library) is a free library with thousands of image functions. Install it once, from a terminal (Command Prompt on Windows): pip install opencv-python Then, in every Python file that uses it: import cv2 cv2 is the module name you will write in front of every OpenCV function. These are the ones this lecture uses: Function What it does --- --- cv2.imread() loads an image from a file cv2.imshow() displays an image in a window cv2.resize() changes an image''s size cv2.cvtColor() converts between colour formats cv2.GaussianBlur() smooths an image cv2.equalizeHist() improves contrast automatically OpenCV programs open windows on your screen, so they run on your own computer, not in the browser. The programs on these pages show their recorded output instead of a Run button. The image used in these labs This is street.png , 960 pixels wide and 540 pixels tall. Download it with the link under the picture and save it in the same folder as your Python file . You can also use a photo of your own: change the file name in the code. Step 1: read and show an image import cv2 img = cv2.imread("street.png") cv2.imshow("Original Image", img) cv2.waitKey(0) cv2.destroyAllWindows() What each line does Line What it does --- --- img = cv2.imread("street.png") loads the image from the file into the variable img cv2.imshow("Original Image", img) displays img in a window whose title is "Original Image" cv2.waitKey(0) waits until you press any key; 0 means "wait forever" cv2.destroyAllWindows() closes all the windows OpenCV opened Without waitKey() , the window would open and close in a blink, or stay grey and frozen: OpenCV only draws its windows while it waits for a key. The window it opens Press any key while the window is selected to close it and end the program. Did the image load? If the file name is wrong, or the file isn''t in the same folder, imread() does not stop with an error. It returns None , and the program fails one line later with a confusing message. Check straight after reading. (OpenCV may also print its own warning line about the file.) import cv2 img = cv2.imread("stret.png") if img is None: print("Could not read the image. Check the file name and folder.") else: print("Loaded:", img.shape) Step 2: images are arrays img is a NumPy array : a grid of numbers with a shape . Print the shape to learn the image''s size: print(img.shape) For street.png it prints (540, 960, 3) : Number Meaning --- --- 540 height : the number of rows of pixels 960 width : the number of columns 3 colour channels : three numbers per pixel The order is always height × width × channels . Height comes first. Printing the properties import cv2 img = cv2.imread("street.png") print(img.shape) height, width, channels = img.shape print("Width:", width) print("Height:", height) print("Channels:", channels) OpenCV uses BGR, not RGB The three channels are B lue, G reen and R ed, in that order . Most other tools say RGB, so OpenCV''s order surprises everyone once: Channel Colour --- --- 0 B: blue 1 G: green 2 R: red So in OpenCV (0, 0, 255) is red and (255, 0, 0) is blue . The next lesson looks at single pixels in detail. Student task 1: try it yourself Write this in your own Python file: 1. Choose any image (a photo from your phone works). 2. Load it with OpenCV. 3. Display it. 4. Print its shape. Then answer: What is the width? What is the height? How many channels? Remember that the shape lists the height first. Check yourself 1280 pixels. The shape is (height, width, channels), so the height is 720 and the width is 1280. Common mistakes - The image is not in the same folder. imread() returns None ; check with if img is None: . - Forgetting waitKey() . The window flashes or stays grey. Always call cv2.waitKey(0) after imshow() . - Reading the shape as width × height. It is height × width × channels . - Expecting RGB. OpenCV stores BGR : red is (0, 0, 255) . - Naming your own file cv2.py . Then import cv2 imports your file instead of OpenCV. Give your files other names. Key takeaways - Install with pip install opencv-python ; use it with import cv2 . - imread loads, imshow displays, waitKey waits for a key, destroyAllWindows closes. - imread returns None for a missing file, so check it. - img.shape is (height, width, channels) , and colour images are stored as BGR .');
INSERT INTO lessons (id, course_id, topic_id, slug, title, description, lecture_number, sort_order, difficulty, objectives, prerequisites, reading_minutes, status, published_at, updated_at) VALUES ('les_c2fc8d3dcbc2d6be', 'course_computer_vision', 'top_95f2507dfdad3472', 'digital-images-and-pixels', 'Digital images and pixels', 'See what an image really is to a computer, a grid of numbers, and use OpenCV to read an image, check its size and read or change single pixels.', 2, 4, 'easy', '["Describe an image as a grid of pixels, each holding numbers","Read an image with cv2.imread and check its shape and data type","Read one pixel and explain OpenCV''s BGR channel order","Change a block of pixels with slicing and save the result"]', '["Getting started with OpenCV","Python Lists and indexing (Programming Applications course)"]', 6, 'published', 1792022400000, 1792022400000) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET topic_id = excluded.topic_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lecture_number = excluded.lecture_number, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objectives = excluded.objectives, prerequisites = excluded.prerequisites, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = excluded.status, published_at = coalesce(lessons.published_at, excluded.published_at), updated_at = excluded.updated_at;
