een Circle center (150, 360), radius 50 Why do all three circles use x = 150 ? Because 150 is halfway between 75 and 225, the left and right sides of the body, so every light sits in the middle of it. Order matters: the body is drawn first. If it were drawn last, the black rectangle would cover the lights. Mini project: draw a house This time there is no solution code, on purpose: design it yourself. Draw a house in a 500 × 400 window titled My House , using: Object Draws the… --- --- Rectangle house body Polygon roof Rectangle door Rectangles windows Circle sun Text title Line ground Use Points for every position, and colours for everything. Plan the coordinates on paper first, and draw back to front : the body and roof before the door and windows, so they appear on top. Bonus: move the sun across the sky with sun.move(-50, 0) . One possible house This is one possible answer. Yours can look completely different: other colours, a bigger house, two suns. What matters is that every part of the picture is an object you created and drew. Debugging graphics programs Five mistakes cause most broken graphics programs. When something goes wrong, check them in order: Common mistake What to check --- --- 1. An object is created but not drawn every shape calls .draw(win) 2. Coordinates outside the window x between 0 and the width, y between 0 and the height 3. close() called too early win.close() is the last line 4. Wrong Point coordinates x comes first; y grows downward 5. The import is missing line 1 is from graphics import Bug hunt This program should show a yellow sun. It has four bugs . Use the checklist above to find them before you open the answer. win = GraphWin("Bug Hunt", 400, 300) sun = Circle(Point(600, 100), 40) sun.setFill("yellow") win.close() sun.draw(win) 1. The import is missing. Without from graphics import on line 1, the program stops at once with NameError: name ''GraphWin'' is not defined . 2. The sun is outside the window. Its center x is 600, but the window is only 400 wide. 3. close() comes before draw() . Drawing on a closed window raises GraphicsError: Can''t draw to closed window . 4. There is no win.getMouse() before close() . Even with the other bugs fixed, the window would vanish instantly. The fixed order is: import, create the window, create and colour the sun, draw it, wait for a click, close. The fixed program from graphics import * win = GraphWin("Bug Hunt", 400, 300) sun = Circle(Point(200, 100), 40) sun.setFill("yellow") sun.draw(win) win.getMouse() win.close() The yellow sun Objects and their methods Here is everything from this lecture in one table. Objects are the things; methods are the actions those things can perform, always written after a dot. Object Its methods --- --- win (a GraphWin) .getMouse() , .close() circle .draw() , .move() , .setFill() , .setOutline() rectangle .draw() , .move() , .setFill() , .setOutline() text .draw() , .move() , .setSize() , .setTextColor() click (a Point) .getX() , .getY() Read circle.move(50, 0) aloud: the thing is circle , the action is move . Coming next: classes In these lectures you used objects that someone else designed: GraphWin , Point , Circle , Text . Next, you will learn classes , which let you design your own kinds of objects, with their own data and their own methods. A question to think about until then: what if you wanted a TrafficLight object, with a method called change() that switches the light from red to green? Common mistakes - Drawing back to front the wrong way round. Shapes drawn later cover earlier ones. Draw large background shapes first. - Guessing coordinates. Sketch the picture on squared paper first; guessing numbers and re-running wastes time. - Copying the same object. To draw three lights, create three Circle objects; one object can only be drawn once. - Fixing one bug and stopping. A program can have several bugs, as the bug hunt shows. Run the checklist to the end. Key takeaways - Plan coordinates before coding, and draw back