f it is greater than 30 °C, turn the fan ON. Otherwise, turn the fan OFF. Part Answer --- --- Input the temperature T Processing compare T with 30 Output Fan ON or Fan OFF Problem: calculate the average of three numbers. Part Answer --- --- Input the three numbers N1 , N2 , N3 Processing add them, then divide the sum by 3 Output the average An everyday algorithm: making tea You don''t need a computer to think in steps. Here is making a cup of tea written as an algorithm: 1. Start. 2. Fill the kettle with water. 3. Switch the kettle on and wait until the water boils. 4. Put a tea bag in a cup. 5. Pour the hot water into the cup. 6. Wait 3 minutes, then take the tea bag out. 7. If you want sugar, add it and stir. 8. End. Notice that the order matters (you can''t pour before the water boils), and that step 7 is a decision : it only happens when the answer is yes. Programs work the same way. Testing finds a mistake This program should print the average of three numbers. We test it with 3 , 6 and 9 . We know the answer should be 6 , because (3 + 6 + 9) / 3 = 18 / 3 = 6. #include <iostream> using namespace std; int main() { float n1, n2, n3; cin >> n1 >> n2 >> n3; float average = n1 + n2 + n3 / 3; cout << "Average = " << average << endl; return 0; } Debug and refine The test failed: we expected 6 but got 12 . Now we debug : we look for the cause. In C++, as in maths, division happens before addition. So the line calculates 3 + 6 + (9 / 3) , which is 3 + 6 + 3 = 12 . Only n3 was divided. The fix is to add brackets so the sum is worked out first: (n1 + n2 + n3) / 3 . The refined program Same test, same input, and now the output matches what we expected. #include <iostream> using namespace std; int main() { float n1, n2, n3; cin >> n1 >> n2 >> n3; float average = (n1 + n2 + n3) / 3; cout << "Average = " << average << endl; return 0; } Check yourself Input: the mark. Processing: compare the mark with the pass mark (for example 50). Output: PASS or FAIL . Not necessarily. One test only shows it works for that one input. Test several cases, including the edges, such as the exact pass mark. Key takeaways - Understand the problem, design an algorithm, then implement and test. - Always start by finding the input , the processing and the output . - An algorithm is a list of ordered steps; some steps are decisions. - Testing with values you can check by hand shows when the code is wrong; then debug, fix and test again.');
INSERT INTO lessons (id, course_id, topic_id, slug, title, description, lecture_number, sort_order, difficulty, objectives, prerequisites, reading_minutes, status, published_at, updated_at) VALUES ('les_ae371952c0c968ae', 'course_programming_fundamentals', 'top_e6268b080b4bf2c7', 'algorithms-steps-flowcharts-pseudocode', 'Algorithms: steps, flowcharts and pseudocode', 'Describe one algorithm in three ways, as plain steps, as a flowchart and as pseudocode, with two worked examples, the average of three numbers and a temperature-controlled fan.', 1, 3, 'easy', '["Say what an algorithm is","Describe the same algorithm as plain steps, a flowchart and pseudocode","Recognise the five flowchart symbols","Write comparison symbols the way C++ needs them"]', '["From problem to program: a method that works"]', 6, 'published', 1791504000000, 1791504000000) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET topic_id = excluded.topic_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lecture_number = excluded.lecture_number, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objectives = excluded.objectives, prerequisites = excluded.prerequisites, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = excluded.status, published_at = coalesce(lessons.published_at, excluded.published_at), updated_at = excluded.updated_at;
DELETE FROM lesson_sections WHERE course_id = 'course_programming_fundamentals' AND lesson_id = 'les_ae371952c0c968ae';
