urly braces { } , and ELSE becomes else . This time the mark typed was 41 . #include <iostream> using namespace std; int main() { int mark; cout << "Enter mark: "; cin >> mark; if (mark >= 50) { cout << "PASS" << endl; } else { cout << "FAIL" << endl; } return 0; } Worked example: find the larger number Problem: read two numbers A and B. Display the larger number. - Input: A, B - Decision: A B ? - Output: the larger number Example run: A = 17, B = 42. 17 42 is FALSE, so B is printed: 42 . Think deeper: what if A = B? Then A B is FALSE, so B is printed. That is still correct, because both values are equal. Larger number as a flowchart Every decision has exactly two exits: YES and NO. Larger number in C++ The user typed 17 42 on one line. #include <iostream> using namespace std; int main() { int A, B; cout << "Enter A and B: "; cin >> A >> B; if (A > B) { cout << "Larger: " << A << endl; } else { cout << "Larger: " << B << endl; } return 0; } Worked example: even or odd Problem: read an integer N. Decide whether it is even or odd. - Input: N - Processing: N % 2 - Output: EVEN or ODD Key idea: % gives the remainder after dividing by 2. Group the items in pairs: if every item has a partner, the remainder is 0 and the number is even. If one item is left over, the remainder is 1 and the number is odd. - N = 8: 8 % 2 = 0 , so EVEN. - N = 7: 7 % 2 = 1 , so ODD. Even or odd as a flowchart The only new idea is the condition N % 2 == 0 . Note the == : it compares, while ← stores. Even or odd in pseudocode The user typed 7 . BEGIN INPUT N IF N % 2 == 0 THEN PRINT "EVEN" ELSE PRINT "ODD" END IF END = instead of == in a condition This program should print FAIL, because the mark is 30. But the condition says mark = 50 , with one = . That stores 50 in mark instead of comparing, and a value that isn''t 0 counts as TRUE. So it prints PASS, and mark is now 50. With warnings turned on, g++ notices and suggests brackets, which is its way of asking "did you mean == ?". #include <iostream> using namespace std; int main() { int mark = 30; if (mark = 50) { cout << "PASS" << endl; } else { cout << "FAIL" << endl; } cout << "mark is now " << mark << endl; return 0; } Check yourself int N = 0; if (N % 2 == 0) { cout << "EVEN" << endl; } else { cout << "ODD" << endl; } EVEN 0 % 2 is 0 (nothing is left over), so 0 is even. 50. With , 50 50 is FALSE, so a mark of exactly 50 would FAIL. That is why the boundary is always one of your test cases. Key takeaways - A decision makes two possible paths. Only one of them runs each time. - In a flowchart, a decision is a diamond with exactly two exits, and the paths join again before End. - Pseudocode: IF condition THEN … ELSE … END IF . C++: if (condition) { … } else { … } . - Test the boundary: with = , the boundary value is included. - N % 2 == 0 means N is even. Use == to compare; = stores.');
INSERT INTO lessons (id, course_id, topic_id, slug, title, description, lecture_number, sort_order, difficulty, objectives, prerequisites, reading_minutes, status, published_at, updated_at) VALUES ('les_9cb99428c03bf192', 'course_programming_fundamentals', 'top_e519989412df8f8b', 'loops-and-the-while-loop-in-pseudocode', 'Repetition: the WHILE loop in pseudocode', 'Repeat steps without copying them. Learn the three parts of every loop, write WHILE loops in pseudocode, trace them iteration by iteration, and see why a missing update never stops.', 3, 10, 'easy', '["Explain why programs need repetition","Name the three parts of a loop, initialization, condition and update","Write and trace a WHILE loop in pseudocode","Count iterations and condition checks","Spot the missing update that makes an infinite loop"]', '["Decisions: IF … THEN … ELSE"]', 9, 'published', 1791504000000, 1791504000000) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET topic_id = excluded.topic_id, slug = excluded.slug, title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lecture_number = excluded.lecture_number, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, objectives = excluded.objectives, prerequisites = excluded.prerequisites, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = excluded.status, published_at = coalesce(lessons.published_at, excluded.published_at), updated_at = excluded.updated_at;
