Thank you: 7
', '{"notes":[{"line":6,"match":"do","text":"the body comes first","shape":"circle","color":"sky"},{"line":9,"match":"cin >> number;","text":"read, then check","shape":"square","color":"sky"},{"line":11,"match":"number <= 0","text":"not positive? ask again","shape":"square","color":"yel"},{"line":11,"match":";","text":"the ; is required here","shape":"tri","color":"coral"}],"strips":[]}', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 3, 1791504000000);
INSERT INTO lesson_sections (id, course_id, lesson_id, kind, title, body_md, code, code_language, runnable, sample_input, expected_output, annotations, image_key, image_alt, image_width, image_height, sort_order, updated_at) VALUES ('sec_7a2644ff46f09f49', 'course_programming_fundamentals', 'les_9962b1823b489bb3', 'code', 'while vs do-while', 'Same variable, same condition, different result. Predict both outputs before you look.

- The `while` loop checks `10 < 5` **first**. It is FALSE, so the body is skipped completely and nothing is printed.
- The `do-while` loop prints 10 **first**, then checks `10 < 5`. It is FALSE, so it stops after one pass.

(Neither loop has an update. That is only safe here because the condition is FALSE from the start.)', '#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
    int x = 10;

    cout << "while prints: [";
    while (x < 5)
    {
        cout << x;
    }
    cout << "]" << endl;

    cout << "do-while prints: [";
    do
    {
        cout << x;
    }
    while (x < 5);
    cout << "]" << endl;
    return 0;
}', 'cpp', 1, '', 'while prints: []
do-while prints: [10]
', '{"notes":[{"line":8,"match":"x < 5","text":"checked first: FALSE","shape":"square","color":"yel"},{"line":10,"match":"cout << x;","text":"skipped completely","shape":"tri","color":"coral"},{"line":17,"match":"cout << x;","text":"runs once before the check","shape":"circle","color":"green"},{"line":19,"match":"while (x < 5);","text":"checked after","shape":"square","color":"yel"}],"strips":[]}', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 4, 1791504000000);
INSERT INTO lesson_sections (id, course_id, lesson_id, kind, title, body_md, code, code_language, runnable, sample_input, expected_output, annotations, image_key, image_alt, image_width, image_height, sort_order, updated_at) VALUES ('sec_75fc08c2f2b4c55e', 'course_programming_fundamentals', 'les_9962b1823b489bb3', 'explanation', 'Choosing the right loop', 'Any loop can be rewritten as any other, so the question is which one is **clearest** for the problem.

| | `while` | `for` | `do-while` |
|---|---|---|---|
| Use when | the number of repetitions is not clearly known | the number of repetitions is known | the body must run at least once |
| Condition checked | before the body | before the body | after the body |
| Body runs at least | 0 times | 0 times | 1 time |
| Example | read numbers until the user types 0 | print 1 to 10, read 5 sensor values | ask for input, then check it is valid |

The rule of thumb:

- **Count known** → `for`
- **Count unknown** (repeat until something happens) → `while`
- **Must run once** (show a menu, ask for input) → `do-while`', NULL, NULL, 0, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 5, 1791504000000);
INSERT INTO lesson_sections (id, course_id, lesson_id, kind, title, body_md, code, code_language, runnable, sample_input, expected_output, annotations, image_key, image_alt, image_width, image_height, sort_order, updated_at) VALUES ('sec_8742f29e1788286d', 'course_programming_fundamentals', 'les_9962b1823b489bb3', 'note', 'Check yourself', '::: check Which loop fits best? (a) Print a multiplication table from 1 to 12. (b) Keep reading prices until the user types 0. (c) Show a menu and ask for a choice, and show it again if the choice is invalid.
???
(a) `for`: the count, 12, is known. (b) `while`: we don''t know how many prices will come before the 0. (c) `do-while`: the menu must be shown at least once before we can check the choice.
:::

::: check How many times does this body run?
```cpp
int n = 0;
do
{
    cout << "Hi" << endl;
    n++;
}
while (n > 5);
```
???
```output
Hi
```
Once. The body runs first and prints `Hi`, then `n` is 1 and `1 > 5` is FALSE, so the loop stops.
:::', NULL, NULL, 0, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 6, 1791504000000);
INSERT INTO lesson_sections (id, course_id, lesson_id, kind, title, body_md, code, code_language, runnable, sample_input, expected_output, annotations, image_key, image_alt, image_width, image_height, sort_order, updated_at) VALUES ('sec_a5b0a17aaf7b8443', 'course_programming_fundamentals', 'les_9962b1823b489bb3', 'common_mistakes', 'Semicolons in the wrong place', 'The two `while` lines look alike but need opposite rules:

- In a **do-while**, the semicolon after `while (condition)` is **required**. Without it, g++ stops with `error: expected '';'' before …`.
- In a normal **while**, a semicolon after `while (condition)` is a **bug**: it becomes an empty body, and the real body never repeats.

```cpp
do { … } while (x < 5);    // correct: do-while ends with ;
while (x < 5) { … }        // correct: no ; after while ( )
while (x < 5); { … }       // bug: the ; is the whole loop
```', NULL, NULL, 0, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 7, 1791504000000);
INSERT INTO lesson_sections (id, course_id, lesson_id, kind, title, body_md, code, code_language, runnable, sample_input, expected_output, annotations, image_key, image_alt, image_width, image_height, sort_order, updated_at) VALUES ('sec_e2f8490f01f0a5a5', 'course_programming_fundamentals', 'les_9962b1823b489bb3', 'key_takeaways', 'Key takeaways', '- `do-while` runs the body first and checks the condition after it.
- Its body always runs at least once; `while` and `for` bodies can run zero times.
- `while (condition);` ends a do-while with a semicolon; a normal while has none.
- Count known → `for`. Count unknown → `while`. Must run once → `do-while`.', NULL, NULL, 0, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 8, 1791504000000);
