INSERT INTO search_index (course_id, entity_type, entity_id, url, title, body) VALUES ('course_programming_fundamentals', 'lesson', 'les_b34272f68889dda4', '/courses/programming-fundamentals/lectures/loops-with-decisions-and-debugging', 'Loops with decisions, and debugging loops', 'Put an if/else inside a for loop to control a fan from five temperature readings, then learn the six loop mistakes to check first and fix a loop that counts the wrong way. Combine a for loop with an if/else decision Explain why the decision is made again on every repetition Recognise six common loop mistakes and what each one causes Trace a broken loop to find its bug Computer engineering example: a temperature sensor loop Read the temperature 5 times. Above 30 → Fan ON. Otherwise → Fan OFF. - The for loop repeats exactly 5 times. - The if / else decision from Lecture 3, now in C++ syntax, decides every time. In a real device the reading would come from a sensor instead of cin . Here the user typed 28, 31.5, 30, 35 and 22. #include <iostream> using namespace std; int main() { float temperature; for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) { cout << "Enter temperature: "; cin >> temperature; if (temperature > 30) { cout << "Fan ON" << endl; } else { cout << "Fan OFF" << endl; } } return 0; } Walk through the readings Reading temperature 30 ? Output --- --- --- 28 FALSE Fan OFF 31.5 TRUE Fan ON 30 FALSE Fan OFF 35 TRUE Fan ON 22 FALSE Fan OFF 30 gives Fan OFF, because 30 30 is FALSE. If the fan should also turn on at exactly 30, the condition must be temperature = 30 . How the logic connects Input, then the decision: TRUE goes to Fan ON, FALSE goes to Fan OFF. Both paths meet again at the update, and the yellow arrow repeats everything for i = 1 to 5. The decision is inside the loop, so it is made fresh for every reading. Six common loop mistakes When a loop misbehaves, check these first. Mistake Code What happens --- --- --- Missing update while (i <= 5) { cout << i; } Infinite loop: i never changes. Wrong condition for (int i = 1; i < 5; i++) Too few (or too many) iterations: this prints 1 to 4 only. = instead of == while (x = 5) Stores 5 in x instead of comparing. while (x == 5) compares. Wrong starting value int i = 0; while (i <= 5) … Off-by-one: 0 to 5 is six numbers, not five. Missing braces while (i <= 5) then cout << i; then i++; Only the first statement after while is in the loop, so i++ never runs: infinite loop. Extra semicolon while (i <= 5); { cout << i; i++; } The ; is the whole loop body, so the real body never repeats; the loop never ends. Off-by-one errors come from the start value or from < versus <= . Wrong condition: one too few The goal was 1 to 5, but < stops before 5. #include <iostream> using namespace std; int main() { for (int i = 1; i < 5; i++) { cout << i; } cout << endl; return 0; } Wrong starting value: one too many The goal was five numbers, but starting at 0 gives six. #include <iostream> using namespace std; int main() { int i = 0; while (i <= 5) { cout << i; i++; } cout << endl; return 0; } Find the bug The goal is to print 12345 . What is wrong? Trace it on paper first. (No OUTPUT box: this loop never finishes.) #include <iostream> using namespace std; int main() { for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i--) { cout << i; } return 0; } The answer i-- makes i smaller: 1 → 0 → -1 → -2 → … i is always less than or equal to 5, so the condition i <= 5 stays TRUE and the loop never ends. The update moves away from the stopping condition, just like the x++ example in the while lecture. Fix: i++ . Then the loop prints 12345 and stops when i becomes 6. The fixed loop #include <iostream> using namespace std; int main() { for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) { cout << i; } cout << endl; return 0; } The habit to build This is the process to follow for every problem: Problem → Pseudocode → C++ code → Test → Debug Understand the problem, plan it in pseudocode, write the C++, test it with real values, and debug with a trace table when the output is wrong. Key takeaways (Lecture 4) 1. Pseudocode logic can be translated directly into C++. 2. while checks the condition before each repetition. 3. for combines initialization, condition and update. 4. do-while always executes at least once. 5. Counters control repetition. 6. Accumulators store running totals. 7. Always make sure the loop can stop.');
